// grammar.ebnf — the flow language, in the notation the Go specification uses.
//
// Name = Expression .   production, terminated by a period
// |                     alternation
// ( )                   grouping
// [ ]                   option, zero or one
// { }                   repetition, zero or more
// "..."                 terminal
//
// THIS FILE IS THE SOURCE OF TRUTH FOR THE LANGUAGE'S SHAPE. The parser in this
// package implements it by recursive descent, and the test suite drives a
// recognizer straight off these productions and requires it to agree with the
// parser in both directions across the corpora. A form that is not written here
// is not in the language.
//
// START SYMBOL
//
//   File
//
// LEXICAL TERMINALS
//
// These seven are produced by the lexer and are opaque to the notation: the
// grammar names them but never spells them out, because their shape is a
// scanning rule rather than a production.
//
//   ident        a name: a letter, an underscore or any non-ASCII byte, followed by more of the same or digits
//   string       a double-quoted literal, backslash escapes honored, may not cross a line break
//   number       a decimal literal with an optional fractional part
//   newline      a line break; a run of blank lines collapses to one, and horizontal whitespace is never a token
//   noteText     a raw note body, opened and closed by a triple double-quote, verbatim, with no escapes at all
//   goSpan       opaque Go text, bracket-depth tracked, ending FOUR WAYS, all at depth zero: at a stop token, at a clause
//                keyword, at a newline, or at a `{` SEPARATED from the expression before it by a space or a tab; NON-EMPTY
//                (an empty span is not a goSpan: `over` and `checkpoint` are the clauses this decides, since a clause written
//                 bare at end of line, or followed straight by another clause keyword, otherwise scans as a well-formed empty span)
//                THE ADJACENCY RULE is what lets a clause carrying an operand sit LAST before a switch body. An ADJACENT `{`
//                is part of the expression and nests as before, so `checkpoint machine.GobCodec[Order]{}` stays whole; a
//                SEPARATED `{` is a block's brace and ends the span. Adjacency rather than parse-completeness, because
//                `machine.GobCodec[Order]` is itself a valid Go expression and "does it parse yet" cannot tell the two braces
//                apart — what tells them apart is that gofmt writes a composite literal's brace adjacent and a block's spaced.
//                THE ESCAPE: a func literal's body brace is always written spaced, so a BARE func-literal operand ends its span
//                early and is refused with a diagnostic naming the route out — parenthesize it, `(func() T { ... })` or
//                `(func() T { ... })()`, which nests the body brace at depth one and is writable in every position.
//                QUOTED LITERALS ARE OPAQUE: a brace inside a string or a rune is text, never a bracket, so
//                `over pubsub.Topic("a{b")` is a well-formed operand.
//   goFuncSpan   a func declaration's parameters, results and body as one span, brace balanced, skipping Go's literals and comments
//
// `number` IS REFERENCED BY NO PRODUCTION BELOW, and that is not an omission.
// The lexer produces number tokens, but every position where a numeric literal
// can appear — a const value, a param default, a switch arm, a Go type's array
// bound — sits inside an opaque goSpan, so the notation never sees one.
//
// PARSER-ONLY RULES
//
// Four rules the parser enforces that this notation cannot express. Each names
// the fixture that gates it, and that naming is load bearing rather than tidy:
// the conformance suite derives its recognizer-versus-parser exemption set by
// reading these lines, so a rule whose gate is missing reds instead of silently
// widening the exemption.
//
// The completeness of this list is a review duty the checks cannot discharge.
// They verify that each LISTED rule is annotated; they cannot tell you about a
// rule nobody has thought of yet. The list grew from two to three when the
// headless-statement case was found, and from three to four when the arrow case
// was derived — each time by asking the same question: what does the notation
// admit that the parser must refuse?
//
// IT THEN GREW TO FIVE AND CAME BACK TO FOUR, and that episode is why the Go
// span's stop set has eight members rather than seven. The conformance recognizer
// proved that the else-last rule this header had claimed the notation expressed
// was not expressed by it at all: an Arm begins with the opaque goSpan terminal,
// and nothing in the notation can constrain what an opaque terminal's scanner
// consumes. It was annotated as a fifth parser-only rule, which was the honest
// record of the language as it then stood. `else` was then RESERVED — added to
// the span scanner's stop set — which makes an else arm unmatchable as an Arm
// and so makes the trailing option genuinely order the two. The rule moved out
// of this list and back into the notation, and the annotation was deleted.
//
// The episode is kept rather than erased because the fifth rule is the reason
// the stop set is what it is, and because it is the one growth that came from a
// GATE rather than from a reading — which is the strongest form of that evidence
// and the reason the gate is worth its cost.
//
//   1. PARSER-ONLY RULE: within one Clauses sequence each alternative may appear
//      AT MOST ONCE. The repetition below is deliberately permissive because the
//      clauses are order-free, and an optional-each restructure would impose an
//      order the parser does not. Gated by testdata/invalid/duplicate-clause.flow.
//
//   2. PARSER-ONLY RULE: a flow-level `on error` or `note` must appear BEFORE the
//      first statement of the flow body. After any statement, a line beginning
//      with `on` or `note` is always that statement's clause. FlowEntry admits
//      both anywhere, so the ordering constraint cannot live in the notation.
//      Gated by testdata/valid/node-on-error-continuation.flow and by
//      TestParseClauseContinuationAcrossNewlines.
//
//   3. PARSER-ONLY RULE: a Statement must follow a FlowDecl. Entry admits
//      FlowEntry directly, so the notation cannot express the containment — File
//      derives Entry derives FlowEntry derives Statement with no FlowDecl in the
//      chain — but a statement before any flow declaration has no flow to belong
//      to and is a diagnostic. Gated by testdata/invalid/headless-statement.flow.
//
//   4. PARSER-ONLY RULE: the goSpan following `on error` must be NON-EMPTY and
//      must NOT BEGIN WITH `->`. Error handling is a declaration, never an edge.
//      The notation cannot express this: tokArrow appears in neither
//      FIRST(Clauses) nor FOLLOW(Clauses), so the stop set for that goSpan
//      contains no arrow and `-> handler` scans as a perfectly good non-empty
//      span terminated by the newline. Gated by testdata/invalid/error-edge-arrow.flow.
//
// Rule 3 and the treatment of `func` point opposite ways on purpose. A FUNC is a
// DECLARATION: it stands alone, belongs to the file, and is legal anywhere at
// file level, which is why funcs are declare-anywhere and hoisted. A STATEMENT
// is a FLOW-BODY MEMBER: it names inputs and produces an output inside a
// traversal, and outside a flow it means nothing. Accepting a headless statement
// would report success for a structure no consumer can interpret, so it fails
// loud at the point of the mistake instead.
//
// Everything else the parser enforces IS expressed below: at-least-one switch
// arm by `Arm { Arm }`, else-last by the trailing `[ Else ]`, IDEMPOTENT's zero
// arity by an operand-free production, continuation-only-before-a-clause-keyword
// by the `[ newline ]` prefixes, and a func body's self-termination by
// goFuncSpan's brace balance.
//
// THAT ENTRY NAMED `checkpoint` UNTIL THE CLAUSE GREW A CODEC OPERAND. Checkpoint
// now reads `"checkpoint" goSpan` and idempotent is the only clause left carrying
// a zero-arity parse rule, which is what clauseIdempotent says in its own words.
// The stale wording outlived the sweeps that should have caught it because it
// wrapped a line boundary: "checkpoint's zero" ended one line and "arity" opened
// the next, so no single-line search for the phrase ever matched it.
//
// ELSE-LAST IS ON THAT LIST ONLY BECAUSE `else` IS RESERVED. The trailing option
// orders nothing on its own: an Arm begins with an opaque terminal, so an else
// arm would match Arm like any other arm value. It is the span scanner stopping
// at `else` that makes an else arm unmatchable as an Arm and leaves the trailing
// option as the only production that can take it. Remove `else` from that stop
// set and this line becomes false again.
//
// EVERY Entry AND FlowEntry ALTERNATIVE ENDS IN `newline`, which makes a
// trailing newline mandatory in every .flow file. A file whose last line has no
// terminating newline does not derive at all.

File       = { newline } { Entry } .
Entry      = Import | ConstDecl | ParamDecl | FuncDecl | FlowDecl | FlowEntry .
Import     = "import" [ ident ] string newline .
ConstDecl  = "const" ident [ goSpan ] "=" goSpan newline .
ParamDecl  = "param" ident goSpan "=" goSpan newline .
FuncDecl   = "func" ident goFuncSpan newline .
FlowDecl   = "flow" ident [ Signature ] newline .
Signature  = "(" goSpan ")" "->" Output { "," Output } .
Output     = ident goSpan .
FlowEntry  = Note | StateDecl | VarDecl | OnError | Statement | newline .
Note       = "note" noteText newline .
StateDecl  = "state" "{" newline { ident goSpan newline } "}" newline .
VarDecl    = "var" ident goSpan [ "clone" goSpan ] newline .
OnError    = "on" "error" goSpan newline .
Statement  = Source | Transform | Branch | Switch | Tee | Sink | Drop | Loop | Send | Use .
Source     = "source" ident goSpan Clauses newline .
Transform  = "transform" ident goSpan From Clauses newline .
Branch     = "branch" ident goSpan From "->" ident "," ident Clauses newline .
Sink       = "sink" ident goSpan From Clauses newline .
Tee        = "tee" ident From "->" ident { "," ident } Clauses newline .
Drop       = "drop" ident newline .
Loop       = "loop" ident newline .
Send       = "send" ident "->" ident newline .
Use        = "use" ident FlowRef From "->" ident { "," ident } Clauses newline .
Switch     = "switch" ident From "on" goSpan Clauses "{" newline Arm { Arm } [ Else ] "}" newline .
Arm        = goSpan { "," goSpan } "->" ident newline .
Else       = "else" "->" ident newline .
From       = "from" ident { "," ident } .
FlowRef    = ident { "." ident } .
Clauses    = { Reads | Writes | Over | Checkpoint | Idempotent | NodeError | NodeNote } .
Reads      = [ newline ] "reads" ident { "," ident } .
Writes     = [ newline ] "writes" ident { "," ident } .
Over       = [ newline ] "over" goSpan .
Checkpoint = [ newline ] "checkpoint" goSpan .
Idempotent = [ newline ] "idempotent" .
NodeError  = [ newline ] "on" "error" goSpan .
NodeNote   = [ newline ] "note" noteText .
